<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Getae</h1><p class="st-meta">514 BCE – 82 BCE (approx.) · capital Helis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Thracian people of the lower Danube, followers of the god Zalmoxis, whose king Dromichaetes captured Lysimachus and whose lands formed the core of Dacia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> names the Getae among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracians">Thracians</a> living south and north of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> marched against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> around 514 BCE; they alone resisted before being subdued. Their territory covered the Danube plain in modern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-romania">Romania</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Getic chiefs and kings ruled from fortified centres; the royal city of Helis is often identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracian-tomb-of-sveshtari">Sveshtari</a>, where a decorated tomb survives. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> says the Getae believed in immortality through the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zalmoxis">Zalmoxis</a>. Grain, horses and trade with Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> cities such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/histria">Histria</a> shaped the economy. Around 292 BCE King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dromichaetes">Dromichaetes</a> defeated and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a>, then released him.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Celtic incursions and pressure from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> fragmented Getic power. About 82 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burebista">Burebista</a> united the Getae and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacians">Dacians</a> into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-dacia">Kingdom of Dacia</a>; Roman writers afterwards used Getae and Dacians almost interchangeably.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/getae.jpg" alt="Getae" width="320" height="260"><figcaption>Image: KDS4444 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Getae_Region.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>514 BCE – 82 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Helis</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Cothelas, Dromichaetes</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Cult of Zalmoxis</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Thracian (Getic)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>, <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Getic kingdom, Kingdom of Dromichaetes</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-298&amp;focus=getae" aria-label="Getae on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5268 579 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Getae lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6140 1251h0M5552 825h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5588 835h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5588 835h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Romania. <a href="/atlas?year=-298&amp;focus=getae">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
